# Change Log — Monitoring and Controlling
#
# PMBOK Guide 6th ed., 4.6 Perform Integrated Change Control.

screen: changes
title: Change Log
phase: Monitoring and Controlling
process: 4.6 Perform Integrated Change Control
order: 80
requires: charter,scope
teaches: |
  Why a baseline turns a change into a decision rather than an edit, and what
  integrated change control actually asks of you. By the end you should be able
  to explain why a change log where everything is approved means the control is
  not real.
before: |
  Something baselined to change. Until the charter is signed and the scope
  agreed, you are still writing the plan, not changing it.
after: |
  Your defence at closeout. Five approved changes explain a missed date
  better than any excuse.
intro: |
  Once something is baselined, changing it is a decision, not an edit. A change
  request is raised, its impact on scope, schedule and cost is assessed, and
  someone with the authority approves or rejects it.

  The log is the record of that. Its value is that it makes the cost of change
  visible: five approved changes explain a missed date better than any excuse.
source: |
  PMBOK Guide, 6th ed., process 4.6 Perform Integrated Change Control, in the
  Monitoring and Controlling process group. The change log is an output, and
  approved change requests are inputs to the executing processes.

field log
  prompt: What has been asked for since the plan was agreed, and what did you decide?
  check column:impact: A change nobody priced is a change somebody else pays for later. Say what each one costs in scope, schedule or quality.
  check column:decided_on: A decided change with no decision date cannot show how long approval took, and cannot say when the baseline moved.
  label: Change requests
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: change
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    Change log (4.6 output). Change requests themselves may be corrective
    action, preventive action, defect repair, or updates.
  why: |
    Every row records what was asked for, what it costs, who decided, and when.
    The impact columns are the point: a change nobody priced is a change
    somebody else pays for later.

    Rejecting changes is a normal, healthy outcome. A log where everything is
    approved means the control is not real.
  if_blank: |
    The baseline erodes silently, and by the end nobody can explain why the
    project looks nothing like the plan.
  example: |
    CR2 | Expand scope to the full lifecycle, not just Initiating | Scope | Sponsor | 2026-09-15 | Approved | Adds four screens; M3 to M5 unchanged
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column change
    label: Requested change
    type: text
    width: 5
  column kind
    label: Type
    type: choice
    options: Scope=accent, Schedule=warn, Cost=warn, Quality=neutral, Defect repair=neutral, Corrective action=neutral, Preventive action=neutral
    width: 2
  column raised_by
    label: Raised by
    type: line
    width: 2
  column raised_on
    label: Raised
    type: date
    width: 2
  column impact
    label: Impact assessment
    type: text
    width: 5
  column decided_by
    label: Decided by
    type: line
    width: 2
  column decided_on
    label: Decided
    type: date
    width: 2
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Proposed=faint, Under review=accent, Approved=ok, Rejected=danger, Deferred=warn
    width: 2

field process
  prompt: Who can approve what, and what goes to the sponsor?
  check filled: Decide this before the first urgent request, or the first urgent request will decide it for you, permanently.
  label: Change control process
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Change management plan (a component of the project management plan, 4.2
    Develop Project Management Plan): how changes are requested, assessed and
    approved, and by whom.
  why: |
    It says in advance who can approve what, so an urgent change does not get
    waved through by whoever happens to be in the room.
  if_blank: |
    Change control exists on paper only, and the first urgent request bypasses
    it permanently.
  example: |
    Anything that moves a milestone date or adds a screen goes to the sponsor.
    Wording and layout changes inside an approved screen do not.
